<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panduranga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Panduranga was the southernmost region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, around modern Phan Rang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phan-thiet">Phan Thiết</a> in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. After the Vietnamese conquest of Vijaya in 1471 it became the last centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> kingship, surviving as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a> and later of the Nguyễn dynasty until its final annexation in 1832.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Panduranga, known to medieval Chinese sources as Bīn Tónglóng and to Vietnamese annals as Phan Lung or Phan Lang, was long an autonomous princedom within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>. A southern dynasty called Panduranga rose in 757, unified Champa and ruled from Virapura, near modern Ninh Phước; Javanese raiders burned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple near Phan Rang in 787. Prince Jaya Harivarman I fled there during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> occupation of 1145 to 1150 and led the resistance from it. According to the Panduranga annals its own line of princes began with Po Sri Agarang, whose reign began in 1195 or 1205, and it kept a tributary tie to the kings at Vijaya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Lê army of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-thanh-tong">Lê Thánh Tông</a> took Vijaya in 1471, Vietnamese sources say a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> general, Bố Trì Trì, fled to Phan Rang, set up his own rule and submitted to Lê Thánh Tông seven days later; the emperor divided the Cham remnants into smaller polities. Scholars now see this less as the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> than as a sign of its many centres. The Cham stopped paying tribute when the Mạc dynasty usurped the Lê throne in 1526, and in the 16th century Panduranga prospered from trade; in 1594 its king sent 400 warships to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> against the Portuguese at Melaka.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a> invaded repeatedly, in 1629, 1653 and 1692. In 1692 Lord Nguyễn Phúc Chu arrested King Po Saut and renamed the land Trấn Thuận Thành, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> revolts that began in 1693 led him to restore the Cham monarchy as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, confirmed by a treaty of 1712. From the early 17th century until 1832 its capital stood at Phan Rí Cửa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> merchants traded with Siam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, Macao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, Pahang, Patani and Makassar, and Cham textiles were widely sold. Most historians agree that the Cham began converting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in large numbers only after the fall of Vijaya, and Pierre-Yves Manguin argues that it came through the Malay world. King Po Rome, who reigned from 1627 to 1651 and had studied in Kelantan, brought the Muslim Cham Bani and the Ahier closer together, and is still venerated by the Cham. By the 1670s European missionaries described a mostly Muslim population and court.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Panduranga (Champa)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wealth was concentrated in port-cities: Simhapura, a riverine port and political centre from about 400 CE, Amarendrapura and Visnupura in the north, Indrapura, Amaravati and Vijaya in the centre, Nha Trang, Virapura and <b>Panduranga</b> in the south, crowded with Chinese, Khmer, Malay, Viet, Arab and Indian traders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hinduism's last places in Southeast Asia are Trowulan, capital of Majapahit, the last great Hindu-Buddhist kingdom of Java, and <b>Panduranga</b>, the last Cham principality, annexed by Vietnam in 1832.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>They encouraged settlement south into former Cham and Khmer lands, absorbing <b>Panduranga</b> and the Mekong delta, and welcomed Chinese Ming loyalists who founded towns such as My Tho.</blockquote><a href="/state/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a></li></ul></section>
